1.624:
Odysseus
(leaving the cave cautiously.)
Silence, ye cattle! I adjure you;

1.145:
Do you trust that these children’s father, who lies dead in the halls of Hades, will return? How unworthily you show your sorrow at having to die, you
to Amphitryon
after your idle boasts, scattered broadcast through Hellas, that Zeus was partner in your marriage-bed and was your partner in children;

1.460:
Ah me! how bitterly my hopes have disappointed me in the expectation I once formed from the words of your father.
Addressing each of her three sons in turn.
To you your dead father was for giving Argos; and you were to dwell in the halls of Eurystheus, lording it over the fair fruitful land of Argolis;

1.1065:
You see how one fortune holds us three, most dear to each other, either to return to our native land, or to die. If I am saved, I will bring you safe to Hellas, so that you may share my fortune. By your right hand, I entreat you, and you, and you;
(addressing different members of the Chorus.)
you by your dear face,

1.208:
DOLON.
A grey wolf’s hide
Shall wrap my body close on either side;

1.631:
ATHENA.
Alone;
Toward Hector’s quarters, as I deem, he plies

1.634:
ATHENA.
No;
Beyond the ordainèd end thou canst not go.
